   157	In this paper, we present LASAPP (Figure 1)—the first approach
   158	to writing static analyses for probabilistic programs in a languageagnostic way. The key insight lies in the fact that while probabilistic
   159	programming languages differ in their implementation and expressiveness, the underlying meaning of these programs remains the
   160	same: a probabilistic model. By using existing program analysis
   161	techniques, like data and control-flow analysis, we can put an abstraction layer on top of classical program properties and formulate
   162	analyses of probabilistic properties in the language of these abstractions at a higher level. Our approach makes it easy to add support
   163	for new languages and thus existing analyses written in LASAPP are
   164	immediately available for them. Furthermore, the clear separation
   165	of classical program analyses entails that one can write probabilistic
   166	analysis without having to be a programming languages expert. We
   167	demonstrate that our framework allows the formulation of many
   168	practical and provably-sound analyses.
   169	The main contributions of this work are:
   170	
   171	  - The first language-agnostic static analysis framework for
   172	probabilistic programs (LASAPP).
