   200	
   201	**2.1** **Probabilistic Programming**
   202	
   203	
   204	Probabilistic programming is an intuitive means to specify Bayesian
   205	models as programs. We start by giving a general definition:
   206	
   207	
   208	Definition. _A_ probabilistic program _is a (non-deterministic)_
   209	_program, for which an (unnormalized) weight can be assigned to each_
   210	_possible execution._
   211	
   212	
   213	If the program returns a number, we can think of it as an (unnormalized) probability distribution over the return values. However, in
   214	probabilistic programming the local random variables of a program
   215	are also of great interest as they typically encode how observed
   216	data is believed to be generated. For each run of the program, the
   217	values of these variables are captured in an _execution trace_ . Thus,
   218	probabilistic programs can also be viewed as an (unnormalized)
   219	joint probability distribution over all random variables declared
   220	in the program, or as an (unnormalized) probability distribution
   221	over execution traces [ 2, 23, 38, 54 ]. This distribution may also be
   222	referred to as _program density_ .
   223	In most probabilistic programming languages (PPLs), random
   224	variables of a model and their prior distributions are declared with
   225	special syntax in the form of _sample statements_ . Relating these
   226	variables with program constructs implicitly defines the likelihood
