   272	We can inspect the posterior distribution to find the line that best
   273	fits the data and quantify the uncertainty about our conclusions.
   274	What makes probabilistic programming special is that across
   275	different languages the semantic meaning of the programs remains
   276	the same. The languages differ in syntax and implementations, making trade-offs between the efficiency of inference algorithms and
   277	generality in terms of which models are expressible. However, the
   278	programs written in any language can all be interpreted as mathematical probabilistic models. Thus, the same probabilistic program
   279	analysis is meaningful in several languages and it is desirable to be
   280	able to apply the analysis directly without having to build up an
   281	analysis framework for each language.
