    64	## Setup
    65	
    66	No special hardware is needed for installation.
    67	
    68	Recommendations:
    69	- Hardware: >= 3.5 GHZ dual core CPU, >= 8 GB RAM, and >= 10 GB storage
    70	- OS: unix-based operating system
    71	- Installation with Docker
    72	
    73	### Docker Installation
    74	
    75	Install [docker](https://www.docker.com).
    76	
    77	Build the lasapp image (this may take several minutes):
    78	```
    79	docker build -t lasapp .
    80	```
    81	If the build was successful, run the docker image:
    82	```
    83	docker run -it --name lasapp --rm lasapp
    84	```
    85	
    86	Alternatively, you can load the docker image provided at [Zenodo](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13347681) with
    87	```
    88	docker load -i lasapp-amd64.tar
    89	docker load -i lasapp-arm64.tar
    90	```
    91	depending on your system, which was saved with (Docker version 28.3.0)
    92	```
    93	docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t lasapp-amd64 .
    94	docker image save lasapp-amd64 > lasapp-amd64.tar
    95	docker buildx build --platform linux/arm64 -t lasapp-arm64 .
    96	docker image save lasapp-arm64 > lasapp-arm64.tar
    97	```
    98	Run those images with
    99	```
   100	docker run -it --name lasapp-amd64 --rm lasapp-amd64
   101	docker run -it --name lasapp-arm64 --rm lasapp-arm64
   102	```
   103	
   104	
   105	
   106	### Manual Installation
   107	
   108	Requirements:
   109	- Unix-based operating system
   110	- Python 3.10.12
   111	- Julia 1.9.2
   112	- tmux
   113	- [graphviz](https://www.graphviz.org)
   114	- [Z3](https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3)
   115	  
   116	Install packages:
   117	```
   118	pip install -r src/py/requirements.txt
   119	julia --project=src/jl -e "using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate();"
   120	```
   121	
   122	### Test Installation
   123	
   124	To test the installation, first we run test scripts for both the Julia and Python language server.
   125	```
   126	./scripts/test_servers.sh
   127	```
   128	(If for some reason a segmentation error is reported run the tests individually: `julia --project=src/jl src/jl/test/all.jl` `python3 src/py/test/all.py`)
   129	
   130	If all tests pass, you can start the language servers:
   131	```
   132	# start language servers
   133	./scripts/start_servers.sh
   134	```
   135	
   136	```
   137	# verify that language servers are running
   138	tmux ls
   139	```
   140	You should see two instances `ls-jl` and `ls-py`.
   141	
   142	If you like, you can attach to the servers with one of the following commands and detach with `Ctrl+b d`.
   143	However, this is not required.
   144	```
   145	tmux attach -t ls-jl
   146	tmux attach -t ls-py
   147	```
   148	
   149	Run the test file.
   150	```
   151	python3 src/static/test/all.py
   152	```
   153	If there are no errors (result is OK), installation was successful.
   154	
   155	## Usage
   156	
   157	```
   158	# start language servers if not started already
   159	./scripts/start_servers.sh
   160	```
   161	Run main file:
   162	```
   163	python3 main.py -h
   164	usage: main.py [-h] [-a A] [-v] filename
   165	
   166	positional arguments:
   167	  filename    path to probabilistic program
   168	
   169	options:
   170	  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
   171	  -a A        graph | hmc | constraint | guide-proposal | guide-svi
   172	  --v         if set, source code of file will be printed
   173	  --view      Only applicable for -a graph. If set, model graph will be plotted and displayed. Otherwise, only saved to disk.
   174	```
   175	
   176	You can use all static analyses:
   177	- `-a=graph` model graph extraction
   178	- `-a=hmc` HMC assumption checker
   179	- `-a=constraint` parameter constraint verification
   180	- `-a=guide-proposal` model-guide validation (model >> guide)
   181	- `-a=guide-svi` model-guide validation (guide >> model)
   182	
   183	Example programs can be found at `experiments/examples`.
   184	
   185	Usage examples can be found below.
   186	
   187	After you are finished, you may stop the language servers.
   188	```
   189	# stop language servers
   190	./scripts/stop_servers.sh
   191	```
   192	
   193	## Reproducing the Results of the Paper (Section 5 / Table 2)
   194	Start the language servers and verify that they are running.
   195	```
   196	# start language servers if not started already
   197	./scripts/start_servers.sh
   198	```
   199	### Evaluation
   200	
   201	Statistical Dependency Analysis (Model Graph) and Parameter Constraint Analysis
   202	```
   203	python3 experiments/evaluate_graph_and_constraints.py -ppl turing
   204	```
   205	This will perfrom the Statistical Dependency Analysis and the Parameter Constraint Verifier for 117 Turing programs.  
   206	For each program, the result will be printed: Everything is ok = correct, Warnings produced = unsupported.  
   207	At the end, the summary counts are reported.
   208	
   209	```
   210	python3 experiments/evaluate_graph_and_constraints.py -ppl pymc
   211	```
   212	Performs the same experiment for 97 PyMC programs.
   213	
   214	HMC Assumption Analysis
   215	```
   216	python3 experiments/evaluate_hmc.py
   217	```
   218	Performs the HMC Assumption Checker for 8 Gen programs.  
   219	For each program, the result will be printed: Everything is ok = false negative, Warnings produced = true positive.  
   220	
   221	
   222	Model-Guide Validation Analysis
   223	```
   224	python3 experiments/evaluate_guide.py
   225	```
   226	Performs the Model-Guide Validation Validator for 8 Pyro programs.  
   227	For each program, the result will be printed: Everything is ok = true negative, Warnings produced = true positive.  
   228	
   229	Note: With the model graph analysis we have identified a bug in <a href="evaluation/turing/statistical_rethinking_2/chapter_14_6.jl">chapter_14_6.jl</a>.
